rota lap 4278, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in; advanced by hand from row 3, which this day's shelf had already read twelve times against Earth's six. Its threshold says that when something reads wrong on this row -- a stamp that outruns the live clock -- the lap trusts that first reading and checks it against the witness, because the concrete fact outranks the plausible story. That is the whole lap: a file mtime a day out of place, checked rather than explained away.
think Opened on the card and the thread, then started the cold roster pass in the background and read while it ran.
think Reds first pointed at %574 -- the report card's two readings disagree about what prose is. Reading it, the instrument turned out to have done its work honestly already: the disagreement is measured, both counts are printed, a shadow reading prices the change, and the decision is deliberately left to Keaton. What it says twice is that it names that question on construction/ITINERARY.md rather than settling it.
think The card does not name it. It named it and then shelved it with the landed account on 20260907.221512, so two living citations in a rostered instrument point at a promise the page no longer keeps. No meter reads this: Truth's counted half asks whether a cited path RESOLVES, and construction/ITINERARY.md resolves perfectly. That is %616's shape one artifact over, and its second firing.
think Measured the class rather than asserting it: 84 `**Yours` blocks stand on 67 of 138 landed-account shelves against 13 on the card. A question shelved is a question withdrawn, and nothing distinguishes an answered one from a forgotten one.
think Then chased a missing evidence file the cold pass had announced -- and the chase found the lap's own red. The pass output I had been reading was not my pass. The redirect and the glob in one command line ran in different shells: the run wrote /tmp/incense-cold-429947.txt, and `ls /tmp/incense-cold-*.txt` printed 4172, a file that PID had written on 20260907 at 11:56 and nobody had swept.
think %549 seated the habit as your own root or a mktemp pen. I kept its letter -- $$ is not a constant name -- and threw the identification away at the glob. Uniqueness without identification buys nothing.
obs The elder file was THIS SAME TREE, which is the worse half. A peer's pass disagrees loudly; your own from yesterday agrees on every structural line and differs only in what has happened since. It carried two reds and an absent evidence room, none of them today's. I ran fleet_watch on that strength -- GREEN in 27s -- and was one step from booking `the runner announces evidence that does not exist` as a red against a healthy instrument whose room had simply been swept and rewritten in the day between.
obs So the finding is sharper than misinformation: a stale reading of your own tree manufactures reds. Kin to %616's stale law page commissioning duplicate work.
obs What broke the tie was concrete, in Earth's own order: an announced file the filesystem did not hold, then a directory timestamp before this lap opened, then the file's own stashed_entries=3 naming 20260906-072048 first against six here naming 20260907-183139, then ps -o lstart on the launch PID against date -u.
obs Third firing of one family -- %541 a signal, %549 a redirect, this a glob -- and the third artifact where a lap's own shell reaches past every law the tree can read. So the clause went on the baton's FLEET stanza, beside the other two and for their reason: a command typed in a session is in no file, so no guard reaches it, and a habit is set where habits are set.
obs Named against myself, twice more. I opened with `ls` of the root, which read-scope forbids and MAP.md replaces. And I edited the tree while the cold pass ran, against the card's hold still while it runs -- the runner caught it honestly with tree_moved=yes, and one of the six reds, declared_ceiling, was my own card standing over its bound mid-trim.
obs The pin cost this lap a real trim: my block carries two findings where the elder carried one, and the card closed at 40,956 of 40,960 -- four bytes of slack -- after eight passes of condensing. That is the tax the PINS clause names, paid in full and worth saying out loud.
