status GREEN on the round's own work, with three things named honestly. The hot pass over the staged tree reads **189 guards, 182 green, 4 red, 3 gated, tree_moved=no** -- and it is the round's reading, because the cold pass ran over a tree I was editing (tree_moved=yes) and certifies nothing on its own. Of the cold pass's six reds, four were mine and are closed: declared_ceiling was my card standing over its bound mid-trim; reds_ledger_headline was my new row, regenerated GREEN; reds_citation was the fold recital claiming %614 while its own shelf holds %613, repaired GREEN; dayshelf_merge was this clone never having armed the driver -- install_hooks run, witness GREEN, which is the card's own ask of every peer ship. The hot pass then found a fifth of mine: unshared_citation at shared_max=618 against the number I had spelled on the card -- derived-spine rule 4, quoted in my own block and broken in the same block -- so the card cites the stamp now and the witness is GREEN. standing_equipment reds on its live_group_plant leg under fleet load, which %549's row already names.
status_open The one that stands is **reds_row_present / reds_ledger_monotone at not_monotone**, and none of it is mine: %530 and %592 are each one number bound to two published stamps, and %512 stands on two shelves. Rules 1 and 3 of derived-spine forbid moving either side of a published double, so no lap may repair it -- which is exactly the shape the law warns turns a gate off. Four guards touching the card were re-verified GREEN after the final trim: declared_ceiling, unshared_citation, index_row_bound, reds_citation. Card closed at 40,951 of 40,960.
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- the ledger's spine cannot be made whole by any lap: %530 and %592 are each one number bound to two published stamps, which rules 1 and 3 of derived-spine forbid moving, so reds_row_present reds on every ship every lap for a fault none of them may repair. That is the shape the law itself warns turns a gate off. The card carries %530 as an open door; %592 has joined it, and the question is one word: does a published double get recorded once and cited by stamp thereafter, or does the guard stop counting it.
